# Why this is exciting

This is another case of how Mojo is going in a very exciting direction: it's laying the groundwork for easier memory-safe high-performance code.


This is something that most of the world believes is impossible.


This is coincidentally, the exact sort of thing Vale was chasing. (explain)


This is also what Nick's [Group Borrowing](https://verdagon.dev/blog/group-borrowing#foundation-builds-on-cs-single-ownership) design is trying to achieve.


Ante is also making some exciting strides here.


Even Carbon is looking into this.


Niko Matsakis, the Rust tech lead, has also written about this (link to View Types post).


All these languages are chasing the same thing: easier memory-safe high-performance code. And they're all heading in roughly the same direction: removing complexity.



# Where this could go: Argument Paths

There are still problems to solve, of course.


We've enabled local mutable aliasing, and we have return ref origin paths to help us with local mutable aliasing, but there's a gap: we aren't able to extract out helper functions anymore.


For example, if we start with this:


```rs
@fieldwise_init
struct Wing(Copyable, Movable):
    var span: Int

@fieldwise_init
struct Tail(Copyable, Movable):
    var length: Int

@fieldwise_init
struct Bird:
    var left: Wing
    var right: Wing
    var tail: Tail

    fn get_weight(self):
        return left.span + right.span + tail.length

fn main():
    var bird = Bird(Wing(10), Wing(20), Tail(15))
    ref tail_ref = bird.tail
    tail_ref.length += bird.get_weight()
```


...and we try to split the `main` into two functions:


```
fn main():
    var bird = Bird(Wing(10), Wing(20), Tail(15))
    ref tail_ref = bird.tail
    evolve(bird, tail_ref)

fn evolve(bird: Bird, mut tail_ref: Tail):
    tail.length += bird.get_weight()
```

we unfortunately get this error:

```
fn main():
    var bird = Bird(Wing(10), Wing(20), Tail(15))
    ref tail_ref = bird.tail

    # error: argument of 'evolve' call allows writing a memory location previously readable through another aliased argument
    evolve(bird, tail_ref)
    #     ^~~~~  ~~~~~~~~
    # note: 'bird.tail' value is passed through aliasing 'mut' argument

fn evolve(bird: Bird, mut tail_ref: Tail):
    tail_ref.length += bird.get_weight()
```


As far as I know, this is the only time we can't extract a local scope into a helper function.


In this way, Mojo _code_ can have mutable aliasing, but Mojo _programs_ do not. Mojo programs will largely be shaped like Rust programs, because this benefit is foiled at function call boundaries.


Luckily, there is a way we can express this to the compiler nicely.


"Aha! Is the answer that we make `evolve` take in a single argument `mut bird: Bird`?"

Good question! But no, that's not what I'm talking about. That does work in simple cases, but it doesn't scale well: when we try that workaround on anything larger than a simple function, we incur extra complexity, performance problems, and architectural risks.


Before I give you the real solution, I'll show you those scaling problems with a couple examples: one from a simple game, and one from Google Earth.


Buckle up, because this blog is about to live up to its name!


## Example: A Simple Game

Let's take this example.

In a little roguelike game, we might have a `plan` method that is purely for the `Entity` to re-orient themselves, and figure out what they want to do (but not actually do it yet).


Here's the `plan` method, plus the structs it interacts with.

There's a certain problem with it, which I'll explain below.


```
struct PursueDirective:
    enemy_id: Int
    enemy_last_seen_loc: Location
    path: List[Location]

struct LoiterDirective: pass  # empty

alias Directive = Variant[LoiterDirective, PursueDirective]

struct Entity:
    loc: Location
    is_blinded: Bool
    directive: Directive

struct World:
    entities: List[Entity]
    tiles: List[List[Entity]]


# Entity looks around and thinks and decides their objective
fn plan(read world: World, mut entity: Entity):
    # If there's an existing directive, check it and see if it even makes sense anymore.
    # In which case, set to loiter.
    match entity.directive:
        case LoiterDirective as walk:
            pass
        case PursueDirective as pursue:
            enemy_loc = world.entities[enemy_id].loc

            if can_see_loc(world, entity, enemy_loc):
                pursue.enemy_last_seen_at = enemy_loc
                # Store the path to the enemy. Keep the path around so we
                # have something to follow if we lose sight of the enemy.
                pursue.path = find_path(entity.loc, enemy_loc)
                if len(pursue.path) == 0:
                    # We see them but can't get to them, abort.
                    entity.directive = LoiterDirective()
                if not world.tiles[pursue.path[0]].walkable:
                    # Next step is unwalkable for some reason, abort.
                    entity.directive = LoiterDirective()
                else: # There's a path to the enemy!
                    pass # Keep pursuing!
            else: # Can't see the player.
                if entity.loc == pursue.enemy_last_seen_at:
                    # We reached where we last saw them. Give up.
                    entity.directive = LoiterDirective()
                else:
                    pass # Keep pursuing!
    ...

fn can_see_loc(world: World, entity: Entity, loc: Location) -> Bool:
    return (
        not is_blinded(entity) and
        has_line_of_sight(world, entity.loc, loc)
    )
```


So let's see the problem. Look at the signature again:

```
fn plan(read world: World, mut entity: Entity):
```

That `entity` is _inside_ the `World`. So, Mojo rejects this:

(show error)


The classic Rust solution would be to refactor `plan` to give it *mutable access to the entire World* (`&mut World`) and a handle (`entity_id`) that we can use to find the `Entity` in the `World`.


```
// Entity looks around and thinks and decides their objective
fn plan(world: &mut World, entity_id: usize) {
    let entity_read = &world.entities[entity_id];

    // if there's an existing directive, check it and see if it even makes sense anymore.
    // In which case, set to loiter.
    // TODO: try to get rid of this clone. then we'll have a mut ref supposedly and we can overwrite it
    match entity_read.directive.clone() {
        LoiterDirective => {}
        PursueDirective(existing_pursue) => {
            let enemy_loc = world.entities[existing_pursue.enemy_id].loc.clone();

            if can_see_loc(&world, entity_read, &enemy_loc) {
                // we store path to the enemy. keep the path around so we
                // dont have to recalculate it every turn, just recalculate
                // if we run into something.
                let new_path = find_path(&world, &entity_read.loc, &enemy_loc);
                if new_path.steps.len() == 0 {
                    // we see them but can't get to them. loiter instead.
                    // need to re-fetch because we need to mutate.
                    // Mention: ironically, this is why vale did its gen ref thing.
                    let entity_mut = &mut world.entities[entity_id];
                    entity_mut.directive = LoiterDirective;
                } else if !world.tile_at(&new_path.steps[0]).walkable {
                    // next step is unwalkable for some reason, stop
                    // need to re-fetch because we need to mutate.
                    let entity_mut = &mut world.entities[entity_id];
                    entity_mut.directive = LoiterDirective;
                } else { // there's a path to the enemy
                    // keep pursuing
                    let entity_mut = &mut world.entities[entity_id];
                    entity_mut.directive = PursueDirective(Pursue {
                        enemy_id: existing_pursue.enemy_id,
                        enemy_last_seen_loc: enemy_loc,
                        path: new_path
                    });
                }
            } else { // can't see the player
                if entity_read.loc == existing_pursue.enemy_last_seen_loc {
                    // we're where we last saw them. give up.
                    // need to re-fetch because we need to mutate.
                    let entity_mut = &mut world.entities[entity_id];
                    entity_mut.directive = LoiterDirective;
                } else {
                    // keep pursuing
                    let entity_mut = &mut world.entities[entity_id];
                    entity_mut.directive = PursueDirective(Pursue {
                        enemy_id: existing_pursue.enemy_id,
                        enemy_last_seen_loc: enemy_loc,
                        path: existing_pursue.path
                    });
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

fn can_see_loc(world: &World, entity: &Entity, loc: &Location) -> bool {
    return !entity.is_blinded &&
        world.has_line_of_sight(&entity.loc, loc);
}

// TODO: change from usize to generational_index
```

We needed to hand in a `&mut World` because:

 * We need mutable access to something inside it.
 * We need access to `World` for various functions (`can_see_loc`, etc.).


However, we just introduced two problems:

 1. We just made it slower: the borrow checker forced us to do `let entity_read = &world.entities[entity_id];` to look up the entity. Unfortunate, because the caller likely already had a reference handy.
 2. We made it slower again: the borrow checker forced us to add an extra `let entity_mut = &mut world.entities[entity_id];` lookup on each path because we couldn't use our `entity_read` to mutate.
 3. `plan` now has mutable access to the entire World.


Let's talk about that last one. This is problematic for two reasons:

 * We risk architectural violations: we now risk someone modifying something outside of this entity, if they didn't understand that `plan` is only supposed to modify this single unit's intentions, it's not supposed to modify anything else in the World.
 * It's viral: it forces all of `plan`'s callers to receive a `&mut World`, so they can give it to `plan`.


In other words, it's a viral architectural risk.


Don't get me wrong, the borrow checker is great and a net positive. But this is a very strong drawback.


And unfortunately, Mojo needs to have the same drawback.


...or does it?


Perhaps not!


There's nothing inherently unsafe with the original code. This is one of those cases where the borrow checker just doesn't have the right information, and therefore has to conservatively reject a valid program.


So what is the right information?


It's one simple addition, to tell the compiler what makes it safe: an *argument origin path.*


We'd simply add `[world.entities]` to the `entity` argument:

```
fn plan(read world: World, mut [world.entities] entity: Entity):
```

That's it.


The rest of the function doesn't need to change. I'll show the full function anyway for reference, and then I'll explain how it works below.


```
# Entity looks around and thinks and decides their objective
fn plan(read world: World, mut [world.entities] entity: Entity):
    # if there's an existing directive, check it and see if it even makes sense anymore.
    # In which case, set to loiter.
    match entity.directive:
        case LoiterDirective as walk:
            pass
        case PursueDirective as pursue:
            # convenient: we're not holding an enemy_ref. but if we were,
            # it might be fine because of origin path tracking.
            enemy_loc = world.entities[enemy_id].loc

            if can_see_loc(world, entity, enemy_loc):
                pursue.enemy_last_seen_at = enemy_loc
                # we store path to the enemy. keep the path around so we
                # dont have to recalculate it every turn, just recalculate
                # if we run into something.
                pursue.path = find_path(entity.loc, enemy_loc)
                if len(pursue.path) == 0:
                    # we see them but can't get to them. loiter instead.
                    entity.directive = LoiterDirective()
                if not world.tiles[pursue.path[0]].walkable:
                    # next step is unwalkable for some reason, stop
                    entity.directive = LoiterDirective()
                else: # there's a path to the enemy
                    pass # keep pursuing
            else: # can't see the player
                if entity.loc == pursue.enemy_last_seen_at:
                    # we're where we last saw them. give up.
                    entity.directive = LoiterDirective()
                else:
                    # keep pursuing
                    pass

fn can_see_loc(world: World, entity: Entity, loc: Location) -> Bool:
    return (
        not is_blinded(entity) and
        has_line_of_sight(world, entity.loc, loc)
    )

```


So how does this work? All we did was change the signature:


```
fn plan(read world: World, mut [world.entities] entity: Entity):
```


How did that one addition of `[world.entities]` make this entire thing memory safe?


It's because the compiler now knows two things:

 * The `entity` reference is pointing at one of the entities in `world.entities`. And therefore...
 * If we ever temporarily turn the `entity` reference immutable (easy), then the entire `world` is temporarily immutable.


And suddenly, with that last item there, we've solved the problems that led to the harmful `&mut World` workaround:

 * We have mutable access to the `Entity`. No need for `&mut World`!
 * We have immutable access to `World` for various functions (`can_see_loc`, etc.). No need for `&mut World`!


This is much better, because now our type system is helping us maintain our architecture, instead of working against us.


Okay, but this is just a toy example, let's hear about a real use case!


