## Example: Google Earth

Long ago, in the earlier days of [Google Earth](https://earth.google.com/web/), we were constantly facing state management challenges, because Earth was such a large app with such complex requirements, and we weren't that principled about it.


(And yes, before you ask, we couldn't eliminate this state. This was _inherent_ state. Client-side apps can't avoid inherent state, you just have to be disciplined about how you handle it.)


Some of these problems got so complicated and spaghetti-ish that there were some bugs that took _weeks_ to fix.

These problems seemed to rhyme, and my spidey sense was tingling. I eventually found the common pattern in all of these problems:

 * Presenter A modifies the AppState.
    * Presenter B observes this, and modifies the AppState in response.
 * Presenter A reads the AppState, found it in a completely unexpected state, and crashed.


We then put some constraints on our system [#statemanager] which completely eliminated this entire class of bugs. One of them was this:

> AppState methods could read and mutate the AppState, but could only _read_ the surrounding world.


This was in C++, so we simply gave AppState some const references to the other subsystems.

In Rust, we might have needed the same workaround:

 * Take a &mut App and suddenly have mutable access to *everything*
 * Take a &mut self, and & refs to everything else (very verbose, there are a lot, cant call other methods)

(Or we could take in 20 subsystems in as arguments)


And Mojo would have the same problems.


But with argument paths, we could make a function like this:

```
struct App:
    var document_manager: DocumentManager
    var app_state: AppState
    var network_requester: NetworkRequester
    var lfs: LocalFileSystem
    ... # About 15 more

    fn can_show_document(self, document_id: Int) -> bool:
        return (
            self.network_requester.ready() and
            self.document_manager.ready() and
            self.document_manager.document_loaded(document_id)
        )

fn open_document_view(
    read app: App,
    mut [app.state] app_state: AppState,
    document_id: Int  # TODO: I think this could even be a ref now?
) -> Bool:
    app_state.currently_open_card_id = None
    app_state.legend_is_open = False
    app_state.currently_open_document_id = document_id
    if app.can_show_document(document_id):
        app_state.current_view = CurrentView::Document
        return True
    else:
        return False
```


(This is of course a simplified version of the actual Google Earth logic, [# For example, there were a few more AppState methods that would be called when the `network_requester` and `document_manager` were all ready.] but you get the idea.)


Note how `open_document_view` is reading the `app` with `app.can_show_document(document_id)`, yet it also has a `mut app_state`. This is possible because of the `app` argument's `[app.state]` origin path.


This is much better, because now our type system is helping us maintain our architecture, instead of working against us.


(TODO: doesn't this also enable better error messages?)
